  "text": "Here's the completed function `remove_adjacent_duplicates` that takes a list of integers and returns a new list where all consecutive duplicate elements have been reduced to a single element.\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]  # Start with the first element\n    \n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    \n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- The function starts with an empty list `result`.\n- It adds the first element of the input list to `result`.\n- Then it iterates through the rest of the list.\n- For each element, it checks if it is different from the last element in `result`.\n- If it is, it adds the current element to `result`.\n- This ensures that only non-consecutive duplicates are removed.\n\n### Example:\nFor input `[1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]`, it returns `[1, 2, 3, 4]`.\n\nYou can run this code to test the provided cases using the `unittest` module.",
